WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.150
- [Narrator] Spacecraft tens
of billions of miles away

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:05.310
rely on this network of satellite dishes

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:06.840
to communicate with Earth,

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.850
but this system is in trouble.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:11.700
The Deep Space Network
is operating at capacity

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.920
and can't keep up with increasing demand,

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.450
which experts say could lead

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:17.550
to loss of important scientific data,

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.800
like information about
the sun's magnetic field,

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:21.120
if nothing is done.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.950
- The thought of not having
that constant connection

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:25.590
to our astronauts, to
these key science missions,

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:28.290
is a challenging and a concerning topic.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:29.850
- [Narrator] Here's how
NASA's infrastructure

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:31.440
got to this critical stage

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.333
and what it'll take to fix it.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:37.950
The Deep Space Network or
DSN uses radio frequencies

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:39.570
to send and receive data

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.730
like this image of the
Southern Ring Nebula

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:43.230
from the James Webb telescope,

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.810
or the sounds of dense plasma
(static crackling)

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.460
captured by the Voyager spacecraft

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.710
as it studies interstellar space.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:52.830
The DSN also commands,
tracks, and monitors

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.200
the health and safety
of spacecraft like Juno,

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:56.520
which is studying Jupiter.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.220
- What we do in space transfers
directly to the earth.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.950
Think about the impact that
Apollo had on what we do today.

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:04.680
Camera systems, the technology
transfer into the industry.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:05.670
There were certain fields

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:07.140
that didn't even exist before Apollo,

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:08.460
like software engineering.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.410
- [Narrator] Because a
spacecraft's radio signals

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:12.990
are being transmitted from
billions of miles away,

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:15.960
they're incredibly faint by
the time they reach Earth.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:19.470
That's why the DSN uses these
giant parabolic dish antennas

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.480
and ultra-sensitive receivers.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.370
There's a total of 14 antennas

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:25.320
at three complexes around the world,

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.120
so spacecraft can reach the network

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.350
no matter where they are.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.120
- Each complex has one 70-meter,

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:31.680
which is really, really large,

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.510
it's like most of a football field,

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:36.420
and then a series of 34-meter antennas.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:37.920
- [Narrator] When data
leaves the spacecraft,

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.420
it travels back to an antenna

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:41.520
where it's decoded by the local facility,

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.490
then sent here to the space
flight operations facility

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:46.200
in Pasadena, California.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:47.033
- Okay, copy.

00:01:47.033 --> 00:01:47.866
- [Narrator] Where it's routed

00:01:47.866 --> 00:01:49.650
to the appropriate mission control center.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.550
But strain on the DSN has
been growing for years.

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:55.920
According to a July
Inspector General report,

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.730
demand is exceeding supply
by as much as 40% at times,

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:02.610
and studies show that
number will reach about 50%

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.140
by the 2030s.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:06.420
Because of this, the DSN has been forced

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.850
to prioritize some missions over others,

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.470
which has led to delays in data

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.810
for some lower priority missions.

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:15.600
This happened during Artemis I in 2022.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.100
- [Crew] Lift off of Artemis I.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.870
- [Narrator] Though it
was an uncrewed mission,

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.360
NASA prioritized its access to the network

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:22.980
because it's preparing the agency

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:24.300
to send humans to the moon

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.580
for the first time in over 50 years.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.980
- We tracked Artemis I
like we will track it

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:30.720
when it has humans on board.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:33.240
We definitely do not
wanna lose communication

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:34.530
with astronauts, right?

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:35.430
- [Narrator] As a result,

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:37.200
all other missions that use the network

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.990
lost over 1,500 hours
of data collection time

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:41.040
during that period.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.090
For example, the James Webb
telescope lost about 185 hours

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:46.350
of data transmission time

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:47.730
when it normally would've been able

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:50.520
to immediately send images back to Earth.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.530
Even though James Webb
and the other missions

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:55.080
were able to eventually
make up for that lost time,

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.110
Smith says these delays
could turn into losses

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.130
in important data

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:02.430
if nothing is done to increase
capacity on the network.

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:04.470
Baldwin says he's especially concerned

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:07.680
given the ambitious goals
the agency has for Artemis.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.140
- The human landing, the Gateway system,

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:11.430
the commercial landers.

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:13.830
It's gonna be challenging
to meet all the objectives

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:15.330
of all the things we wanna do,

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:18.090
including science and the
human aspiration portion of it.

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:20.310
- [Narrator] The DSN will
face an even greater strain

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.350
when the network's largest capacity users

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:23.940
will be in the same part of the sky

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:25.680
vying for the same antennas.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.750
- It's very possible
James Webb will take a hit

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:29.250
on their science data collection.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:30.083
The Europa Clipper

00:03:30.083 --> 00:03:31.890
as we build up the Artemis III and beyond.

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:33.540
The Psyche Mission that just launched.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:36.510
Really it's users that
require a lot of DSN time

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:39.060
that takes the most amount of impacts.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.880
- [Narrator] So why is the
DSN in this dire state?

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.740
One reason is that a lot of missions

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:47.310
are lasting much longer
than originally anticipated.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.950
Voyager, which launched in 1977,

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:52.950
is still transmitting data
from beyond the solar system.

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:55.500
- It is still going strong 46 years later.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.330
Our mission load just keeps growing

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:58.710
'cause we've got all the new missions

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:00.150
and then we also have older missions

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:02.220
that continue to operate.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:03.240
- [Narrator] But the big reason

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:04.980
is that the DSN's infrastructure,

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:06.900
some of which was built in the sixties,

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:10.260
has become increasingly
difficult and costly to maintain.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:11.640
- And that cost does increase.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:14.190
We have inflation, supply
chain issues that impact us.

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:16.440
And so there's a list of things
that will impact our ability

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.450
to move out on these antenna builds

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:21.540
and also to maintain our
operational readiness

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:23.010
to support these missions.

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:25.710
- [Narrator] Some major upgrades
also take antennas offline

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:27.150
for months at a time,

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:28.260
which can increase pressure

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:30.780
on the already overburdened network.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.970
- We've had issues of
complexes going down.

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:33.930
That happened on Artemis.

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:36.060
We had a complex go down
and we had to bring back up.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:39.120
We've had axles break in our antennas.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:40.830
- [Narrator] The agency
has also had difficulty

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:42.360
building new infrastructure.

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:44.880
Its 2010 initiative to
build six new antennas

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:47.100
is nearly five years behind schedule.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.080
- When we're talking about
long term, it's challenging.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:50.010
There'll need to be investment

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:53.610
to ensure that we can support
the long-term plans of NASA.

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:55.020
- [Narrator] One potential fix:

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:56.700
a network of smaller antennas

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:58.740
to specifically serve lunar missions

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:01.770
so it can offload those
missions from the DSN.

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:05.520
The first of these antennas
is set to be built by 2026.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:07.140
NASA is also testing the use

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:09.210
of optical communication or lasers

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:11.940
on the spacecraft studying
the Psyche asteroid.

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:14.400
- Psyche has a tech demo on it right now.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:16.920
So instead of sending
radio frequency signals

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:17.760
through an antenna,

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:21.180
it will use a laser to encode data.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:23.460
- Baldwin says the DSN is also leaning

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:25.590
on other space agencies around the world,

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:27.900
borrowing their antenna
networks when needed.

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:30.300
- They did help us with Artemis I.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:32.130
When we had to shift some of the missions

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:34.920
over to other international agencies,

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:35.753
we did that.

00:05:35.753 --> 00:05:39.570
We are gonna explore that for
Artemis II and beyond as well.

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:40.770
- [Narrator] Despite these efforts,

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:43.830
Baldwin says the DSN isn't
out of the woods yet.

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:45.690
- We're concerned, right?

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:47.610
Maintaining schedule is key

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:49.920
and difficult with some
uncertainty in the budget.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:52.170
So I'd be remiss to say
that there's no worry,

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:53.310
that we're all good.

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:54.810
- For the first time in 50 years,

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:56.970
the DSN is gonna be
supporting human space flight,

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:58.860
but we're also supporting
all of these other missions.

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:00.660
So it's a different load on the DSN

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.040
than what we've had recently.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:05.310
- For the public to know that
we are being good stewards

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:06.270
of taxpayer dollars

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:08.610
and ensuring that we can
bring down that investment

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:12.496
that they made into the science
exploration, I think is key.

00:06:12.496 --> 00:06:15.079
(bright music)

